"prof.kids.custody.hint":"<b>Why it matters.</b> For children aged 6–17 and for the year they turn 18 the points depend on custody, not on the parent’s gender — that is how the official Tax Authority simulator counts.<br>A child <b>in your custody</b>: ages 6–17 — 2 points, the year they turn 18 — 0.5.<br>A child <b>not in your custody</b> (living with the other parent): ages 6–17 — 1 point, nothing for the year they turn 18. If you also pay alimony, tick the separate ground below.<br>For children under 6 the table is the same for both parents.",
"prof.kids.widower":"Married to a widower with children under 18? Enter his children in the table above — they give credit points too (the condition is marked on Form 101).",
"prof.kids.defer.hint":"<b>What it is.</b> Niyud nekudot zikuy — in the year she gives birth a woman may defer <b>exactly one</b> credit point to the next tax year: one point less in the birth year, one point more in the year the child turns 1.<br><b>Why.</b> It pays off if you were on maternity leave that year and barely paid tax anyway: the deferred point is not wasted and works next year, when income is higher.<br><b>How.</b> Mark it on Form 101 with your employer (and in the annual return if you file one). Only one point, only for one year.",
